boot: image: add "op-tee" as an alias for IH_OS_TEE

rkbin's mkimage config marks the OP-TEE image in the FIT with
os = "op-tee", while mainline U-Boot's uimage_os[] table only
recognizes the string "tee" for IH_OS_TEE.

This mismatch is silent and does not break the build or the boot
log in any obvious way: common/spl/spl_fit.c loads and decompresses
the OP-TEE image into RAM regardless of whether the "os" string is
recognized, since load_simple_fit() only relies on the load/data/
compression FIT properties.

However, on ATF/TF-A boot paths (common/spl/spl_atf.c,
CONFIG_SPL_ATF=y), the OP-TEE entry point handed to BL31 as BL32 is
found separately, by looking up a FIT loadable node whose recorded
"os" property resolves to IH_OS_TEE (spl_fit_images_find() ->
genimg_get_os_id()). If "op-tee" is not a known alias, this lookup
fails, bl32_entry stays 0, and BL31 is told there is no secure
payload at all:

    bl_params_node->ep_info->pc = bl32_entry ? bl32_entry : 0;

Net effect: OP-TEE is correctly loaded into memory, but never
receives control from BL31. The board boots normally otherwise,
so this failure is easy to miss without explicitly checking for
Trusted Execution Environment support at runtime.

Adding "op-tee" as a second alias for IH_OS_TEE in uimage_os[] fixes
the lookup without affecting the existing "tee" alias or any other
table entry.

--- a/boot/image.c
+++ b/boot/image.c
@@ -105,6 +105,7 @@ static const table_entry_t uimage_os[] = {
 	{	IH_OS_PLAN9,	"plan9",	"Plan 9",		},
 	{	IH_OS_RTEMS,	"rtems",	"RTEMS",		},
 	{	IH_OS_TEE,	"tee",		"Trusted Execution Environment" },
+	{	IH_OS_TEE,	"op-tee",		"OP-TEE" },
 	{	IH_OS_U_BOOT,	"u-boot",	"U-Boot",		},
 	{	IH_OS_VXWORKS,	"vxworks",	"VxWorks",		},
 #if defined(CONFIG_CMD_ELF) || defined(USE_HOSTCC)
